task: lexer-buf-without-concat
agent: opus5-lex
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/lexer-buf-without-concat
worktree: ../okay-wt-lexbuf
spec: docs/benchmarks.md §10 — BACKLOG "lexer-buf-without-concat"

why: after lexer-state-allocation took the positions out (-6.1% B/op),
  ~171 B per input CHARACTER remain, and the scanner's `buf: String`
  grown one character at a time is most of it — quadratic in the
  token's length.

the constraint, already measured and written down: the obvious fix
  (carry the start offset, slice the input at `finish`) CANNOT serve
  the chunked path — `Lex.chunks` holds one chunk and a token may span
  chunks, so there is no input to slice from.

what: PRICE the three candidates before building one, because the
  cheap-looking one may not be cheapest:
   a) a char array in the state, grown by doubling — allocation per
      GROWTH rather than per character, and no input needed;
   b) `Either[offset, chars]` — slice where the input is there,
      accumulate where it is not. Two scanners in one type; the cost
      of that is to be stated before it is built, not after;
   c) `Scan.finish(s, input)` — an interface change that lets the
      element-wise path slice and leaves the chunked path as it is.
  A probe per candidate in the benchmark, B/op FIRST (the target is
  the ~171 B/char), then a quiet box for time if any of them earns it.

gate: it lands only if a candidate shows a real share of the 171 B
  AND the lex/codec/parse suites are green — token identity, spans,
  and the incremental relex laws (`key`, `rebase`) are the contract.
  If none does, the numbers close the item, as they closed
  chunked-lexer-bookkeeping.
